course: everyday
lesson: 212
title: Anger and fears
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Anger and fear are normal feelings that protect us, but we choose what we do with them.
explain: Anger is easiest to calm when you catch it early: a tight chest or a hot face is your signal to pause, and a calm, clear reason is heard far better than a shout.
explain: Fear shrinks with facts, plans and small steps: counting between the flash and the bang shows how far away a storm is, and a meeting place agreed in advance means you know what to do if you get lost.
explain: A girl in Auckland is scared of her vaccination; she tells the nurse, looks away, breathes out slowly and holds her dad's hand, and the sting is over in a second.
explain: When fear comes from a film or the news, talking with an adult helps: films are made up with special effects, and the news shows the worst things, often not the helpers.
fact: Slamming a door can trap fingers, break things and frighten others, so you close it gently and talk once you are calm.
fact: When something is truly unfair, you say calmly what is unfair and what you would like instead.
fact: People listen to a calm, clear reason much more than to a shout.
fact: A tight chest or a hot face are early warning signs of anger, and a signal to pause.
fact: The earlier you catch anger, the easier it is to calm down.
fact: After a frightening film, talking with a parent and doing something calm before bed helps; films are made up with special effects.
fact: Before a vaccination (in Britain often called a «jab») you can tell the nurse you are scared, look away, breathe out slowly and hold a hand; the sting lasts a second and the vaccine protects you from serious illness.
fact: In a thunderstorm you stay indoors away from windows; inside a house you are safe.
fact: Counting the seconds between the flash and the bang shows whether the storm is coming closer or moving away.
fact: Most spiders and insects are harmless; you keep your distance and ask an adult to move one outside, and in Australia some spiders need an adult.
fact: Courage to swim in the deep end grows step by step with a teacher, and only where an adult is watching.
fact: If the news scares you, you ask an adult to explain and limit how much news you watch.
fact: Agreeing a meeting place with your family before a crowd means you know what to do if you get lost; you can ask a staff member or a parent with children for help.
exercise: You are furious and want to slam your bedroom door. What is better? | Close it gently and talk once you are calm | Slam it as hard as you can so they know how you feel | Kick it until you feel better | Lock it and refuse to come out ever
exercise: Why is slamming a door a bad idea? | It can trap fingers, break things and frighten others | It makes the room colder | Doors are designed to be slammed, so it does no harm at all | It makes the anger last longer only for adults
exercise: Your teacher blames you for something your neighbour did. What works best? | Say calmly what is unfair and what you would like to happen | Shout at the teacher until she finally stops and listens to you | Throw your pencil case | Refuse to speak for the rest of the day
exercise: Why does a calm voice work better than a shout? | People listen much more to a calm, clear reason | Shouting is just not loud enough to get through to people | Teachers cannot hear shouts | Calm voices are always right
exercise: Your face goes hot and your chest feels tight during an argument. What does it mean? | Anger is starting, a signal to pause | You are about to catch a nasty cold or the flu | You are hungry | You are winning the argument
exercise: Why should you notice anger early? | The earlier you catch it, the easier it is to calm down | Early anger is stronger and better, so you should let it grow big | So you can shout first | Anger only lasts if you notice it
exercise: You cannot sleep after a scary film. What helps? | Talk to a parent and do something calm before bed | Watch another scary film straight away to get used to it | Stay awake all night to keep watch | Hide under the covers and tell nobody
exercise: You are scared of the needle for your vaccination. What can you do? | Tell the nurse, look away, breathe out slowly and hold a hand | Run out of the room | Stare hard at the needle the whole time so you know when it's coming | Hold your breath until you go red
exercise: There is a thunderstorm and you are frightened. What is safe? | Stay indoors away from windows and keep busy with something cosy | Go outside to watch the lightning | Stand by the window to check the sky | Shelter under a tall tree in the garden
exercise: You count 3 seconds between the flash and the bang, then 8 seconds for the next one. What is happening? | The storm is moving away | The storm is getting closer | The storm is right above you | Thunder is getting faster
exercise: A big spider is on the wall in your bedroom in England. What do you do? | Keep your distance and ask an adult to move it outside | Squash it with your hand | Pick it up to show your friends | Scream and spray it with water
exercise: You are scared to swim in the deep end. What is the best way? | Learn step by step with a teacher, only where an adult watches | Jump in alone to get it over with | Never go in water again | Wait until nobody is looking and try
exercise: The news about a wildfire makes you worried. What helps? | Ask an adult to explain and watch less news | Watch the news all day long so you are always updated | Keep it secret and worry alone | Pack your bag to run away
exercise: Before going to a busy fair, what helps if you are scared of getting lost? | Agree a meeting place with your family | Hold no hands, so you can all move about faster | Go off alone to learn the way | Stay at home forever
exercise: You are lost in a crowd. Who could you ask for help? | A staff member or a parent with children | Anyone at all who kindly offers you sweets | Nobody, just keep walking until you find them | A stranger who asks you to come to their car
test: A boy in Ohio is angry because his sister got a bigger slice of cake. Which response is most likely to be listened to? | "I think that's unfair. Could we cut it equally next time?" | Screaming at her across the table | Grabbing her plate | Slamming the door
test: A girl notices her fists clenching while playing a game she is losing. What could she do? | Take it as a signal to pause and breathe | Clench them harder so the anger goes into her hands | Throw the game on the floor | Keep playing and get angrier
test: A boy is still scared after a monster film. What is true? | The film was made up with special effects | Monsters like that may well come and visit tonight | Watching more will cure it | Talking about it will make it worse
test: Why does a quick vaccination sting matter less than the vaccine? | The sting lasts a second, and the vaccine protects you from serious illness | The sting is the medicine itself | Vaccines only matter for adults | Stings make you stronger
test: During a storm, the time between flash and bang gets shorter each time. What does this tell you? | The storm is coming closer | The storm is going away from you fast | The storm has stopped for now | Lightning is slowing down a lot
test: Which place is safest during a thunderstorm? | Inside a house, away from windows | Under a big tree with thick leaves | On an open football field, away from buildings | At the edge of a lake, by the water
test: A girl in Sydney finds a big spider in her shoe. What should she do? | Not touch it and ask an adult, as some Australian spiders are dangerous | Pick it up to put it outside herself | Squash it with her bare hand | Put the shoe on carefully
test: A boy keeps seeing frightening news about a war on TV and cannot sleep. What helps? | Ask an adult to explain and limit how much news he watches | Watch more to understand everything | Keep the TV on all night | Pretend he is fine and say nothing
test: A girl's family agreed to meet at the big clock if anyone gets lost at the zoo. She loses them. What does she do? | Go to the big clock, or ask a staff member for help | Leave the zoo to look outside | Hide in a bush and wait | Walk home on her own
test: A boy is scared of deep water. Which plan builds courage safely? | Small steps with a teacher, with an adult always watching | Jumping off the end of the pier into the sea, alone and quickly | Going to the deep end with no one around | Avoiding all water for good
test: so mad rn gonna slam my door | Close it gently and talk once you're calm | Slam it as hard as you can to show them all | Kick it as well | Slam it twice so they hear
test: flash then i counted 10 secs b4 the bang, now its 4 | The storm is coming closer | The storm's moving away, nearly gone | The storm's stopped for now | Thunder's getting slower and slower
test: lightning outside, can i watch at the window | Stay indoors away from windows | Go outside so you can see it better | Stand by the window, glass is safe | Open the window wide to hear it
test: got my jab tomorrow and im scared | Tell the nurse, look away, breathe out slowly and hold a hand | Run away when they call you | Keep your eyes on the needle | Say nothing and hold your breath
explain: A boy in Birmingham lost his temper and threw a heavy toy at his brother; it hit him by the eye and he needed stitches, and the boy felt terrible for weeks.
explain: A girl in Vancouver was so afraid of the dentist that she hid her toothache for months; by the time she told anyone the tooth had to come out.
fact: Hitting or throwing things in anger can cause real injuries in a second, even when you did not mean to.
fact: Hiding a fear or a pain usually makes the problem bigger; telling someone early makes it easier to fix.
test: so angry i wanna throw this at him | Put it down, walk away and calm down first | Throw it, he totally deserves it after what he did | Throw something soft instead | Hit the wall instead
test: Why did the Vancouver girl's hidden toothache end with the tooth taken out? | The problem grew while nobody knew | Dentists always take out every bad tooth they see | Toothache always ends that way in the end | Telling someone only makes teeth worse
test: kept having bad dreams but scared to say | Tell a parent, talking about fears helps | Keep quiet so nobody laughs at you about it | Stay awake all night | Watch scary films to get used to it
test: did u hear a kid slammed the door in a rage and chopped his sister's finger right off?? real lesson? | Close doors gently; slamming can trap fingers | Only slam doors when nobody's anywhere near them | It's fine if you're really angry | Doors never hurt anyone
test: apparently a kid got hit by lightning inside his house watching telly, my cousin said. what's probably just a rumour? | That; inside a house in a storm you are safe | That lightning is dangerous for people outdoors in a storm | That storms can be loud | That flashes come before thunder
test: my mate said the vaccine needle goes right into ur bone and some kid fainted for an hour. should i be scared? | No, the sting lasts a second and the vaccine protects you | Yes, it's very painful and dangerous, it goes right into the bone | Yes, so refuse to have it | Yes, every kid faints after it
exam: Identify the principal function of early bodily signs of anger, such as a hot face. | They signal that one ought to pause | They signal that one ought to shout loudly | They have no meaning of any kind whatsoever | They indicate that one is becoming ill
exam: Which of the following constitutes the most effective manner of objecting to a genuine unfairness? | To state calmly what is unfair and what is desired instead | To shout until one is heard | To slam a door to make the point | To refuse to speak at all
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning shelter during a thunderstorm. | One remains indoors away from windows, where one is safe | One shelters beneath a tall tree | One remains in an open field | One stands by a window to watch
exam: Ascertain the information obtained by counting the seconds between the flash and the thunder. | Whether the storm approaches or recedes | The precise temperature of the air at that moment | How long the rain shall last | Whether the lightning was coloured
